eady to implement. I'm scanning through the requirement states to see which SRDs have all their requirements marked as ready, and I notice a big gap—there are several command utilities like format, testutils, cat, nproc, logname, echo, and tac that don't appear in the requirement_states list at all, which means they're either missing SRD definitions or haven't been tracked yet. Looking at the full list of SRDs in requirement_states, I can see there are many more available beyond the initial nine I identified—these additional ones include tr, tail, cp, mv, rm, date, sleep, touch, timeout, shuf, factor, expr, split, csplit, join, fmt, numfmt, od, printf, and various checksum and encoding utilities, plus stat, truncate, link, sync, and chmod. Now I'm mapping out the release structure and checking which commands are ready versus implemented. I can see rel06.0 covers file operations with mktemp and ln ready but not started, while rel06.1 handles environment and identity commands that are all marked ready, and rel07.0 is beginning text processing with sort and tr queued up.\n\nLooking at the priority system, I need to find the earliest incomplete release by checking which ones have unstarted work and valid SRDs in the requirement states — rel00.0 and rel01.1 are blocked because their SRDs aren't available, but I'm scanning through the releases to identify where I should focus next.\n\nContinuing through the releases, most have either all work completed or missing SRDs that prevent me from proposing anything. But rel06.0 stands out — it has mktemp and ln both ready to go, with their SRDs already in the requirement states.\n\nNow I need to verify the weight constraint. Since I can't access requirements.yaml directly, I'll assume each requirement defaults to weight 1 and check if the total stays under 4 for any task I propose.\n\nLooking at the mktemp requirements, I'm seeing multiple groups with several items each—more than the 4-requirement limit per task. I need to figure